h3 { font-size: 13px; margin: 14px 0 4px; }
.when { font-weight: 400; color: #777; }
.earlier { font-size: 11px; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: #777; margin: 18px 0 2px; }
hr { border: 0; border-top: 1px solid #ddd; margin: 16px 0 0; }
</style>
<h2>Asobi Review 1.0 (build 1651) <span class="when">Oct 16, 2026</span></h2>
<ul>
<li><b>ACES 1.3 and 2.0 configs keep your settings.</b> Moving from ACES 1.2 to 1.3 (or back, or on to 2.0), a saved display and view keep their screen: 1.2&#8217;s &#8220;ACES&#8221; / &#8220;Rec.709&#8221; becomes 1.3&#8217;s &#8220;Rec.1886 Rec.709 - Display&#8221; / &#8220;ACES 1.0 - SDR Video&#8221;, for the window, the default view and each file&#8217;s own view.</li>
<li><b>Movies and stills decode correctly under the newer ACES configs.</b> They go in through &#8220;Output - Rec.709&#8221; and &#8220;Output - sRGB&#8221; as in ACES 1.2; configs without those spaces now get them, made from the config&#8217;s own ACES view. Before, the built-in config read a movie darker and tone-mapped it twice. A packaged review proxy made under these configs now declares the same space, so it opens as it was made.</li>
<li><b>The built-in config is now ACES 1.3.</b> With no config set (no $OCIO and nothing in Settings), Asobi uses the ACES 1.3 config built into OpenColorIO, the same as Kura&#8217;s aces_1.3, instead of ACES 2.0. Pictures look as they do under ACES 1.2, not 2.0&#8217;s slightly different rendering.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<div class="earlier">Earlier updates</div>
<h3>1.0 (build 1476) <span class="when">Oct 12, 2026</span></h3>
<ul>
<li><b>DPX sequences play.</b> 8, 10, 12 and 16-bit DPX files open and play like any other image sequence, read as the color space set for DPX in Settings, Color.</li>
<li>A tall floating Playlist with thumbnails could stop the app. Fixed.</li>
<li>Small fixes.</li>
</ul>
<h3>1.0 (build 1441) <span class="when">Oct 11, 2026</span></h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Network is in the Review menu.</b> Review ▸ Network; it was under Window. It is also in the right-click Review menu.</li>
<li><b>A playlist on a network drive no longer slows the window.</b> Whether each clip's file is there is checked in the background.</li>
<li><b>Locate Media… reads a folder in the background.</b> A line above the toolbar says how far it has got.</li>
</ul>
<h3>1.0 (build 1430) <span class="when">Oct 11, 2026</span></h3>
<ul>
<li>Small fixes.</li>
</ul>
<h3>1.0 (build 1427) <span class="when">Oct 11, 2026</span></h3>
